  abstract = {We are pleased to present this inaugural issue of the Statistical Analysis and Data Mining Journal. This special issue is devoted to the best papers submitted to the Seventh SIAM International Conference on Data Mining that was held in Minneapolis, Minnesota on April 24–26, 2007. The Seventh SIAM International Conference on Data Mining (SDM'2007) continued a series of conferences whose focus is the theory and application of data mining to complex datasets in science, engineering, biomedicine, social sciences, and businesses. These datasets challenge our abilities to analyze them because they are large, and often noisy. Sophisticated, high-performance, and principled analysis techniques and algorithms, based on sound statistical foundations, are required. Visualization is often critically important; tuning for performance is a significant challenge; and the appropriate levels of abstraction to allow end-users to exploit sophisticated techniques and to understand clearly both the constraints and the interpretation of results are still something of an open question. In response to the call for papers, the conference received a record number of papers (302 as compared to 242 last year). After a thorough peer-review process, 36 papers were selected to appear as full papers (about 12%) and 39 papers were selected as poster papers (about 13%). The rest of this special issue is devoted to three of the best papers presented at the conference. On the basis of the recommendation of the },
}

