  abstract = {We studied trajectory management in moving objects database and power efficient resource dissemination in the thesis. A common way of storing spatio-temporal information about mobile devices is in the form of a 3D (2D geography + time) trajectory. For the purpose of efficient storage and processing, the raw trajectories that generated by the mobile devices should be pre-processed before they are stored in moving objects databases. We propose to adopt a technique studied in computer graphics, namely line-simplification, as an approximation technique to solve storage space problem of trajectories. The effectiveness of line-simplification in reducing the size of a trajectories database is verified experimentally. We postulate the query error boundedness desiderata for the distance-function used in line simplification and prove that some function are sound for some types of queries, while others are not. The problem is also addressed for the road network confined trajectories together with the road snapping problem. We propose an alternative representation, called a nonmaterialized trajectory, which addresses both problems by taking advantage of the a priori knowledge that the motion occurs on a transport network. We devise an efficient algorithm to produce a nonmaterialized trajectory at distance at most epsilon from the original trajectory. We consider the problem of inserting a moving object trajectory into an existing R-tree, and process range queries on this tree. Our object},
}

